ion in Aqueous Small Bodies in the Early Solar System (https://pubs.acs.org/acscii/article/8/12/1664/797728/Gamma-Ray-Induced-Amino-Acid-Formation-in-Aqueous)\n  31. Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n  32. Prebiotic aqueous reactions catalyzed by native nickel without hydrogen (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.01.05.697669.full.pdf)\n  33. Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n  34. The evolution of amino acids under asteroidal aqueous alteration - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703724005167)\n  35. Reaction Kinetics of Amino Acid Decomposition in High-Temperature and High-Pressure Water (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/ie020733n)\n  36. Distribution and enantiomeric composition of amino acids in the Murchison meteorite (https://www.nature.com/articles/296837a0)\n  37. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  38. Cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanosulfidic_prebiotic_synthesis)\n  39. Predicting the Decomposition Mechanism of the Serine \u03b1-Amino Acid in the Gas Phase and Condensed Media (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acsomega.3c10496)\n  40. From Amino Acids to \u03b1\u2010Keto Acids via \u03b2\u2010Elimination and Transamination Initiates a Pathway to Prebiotic Reaction Networks - Ter\u2010Ovanessian - 2025 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.202507248)\n  41. Abundant extraterrestrial amino acids in the primitive CM ... (https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Glavin2020%20Asuka1.pdf)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.66\n- Feedback: The graph is connected with no isolated subgraphs, molecule IDs are used consistently, and the overall strategy is well aligned with the serine literature: glycolaldehyde Strecker chemistry and catalyst-gated glycine hydroxymethylation are the right focal routes, and the serine cleavage sink is a useful realism check. The main weakness is network validity. The ground-truth balance check flags rxn_002, rxn_003, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_010, and rxn_011 as unbalanced, while rxn_004 only works after changing to 2 CH2O -> glycolaldehyde. For correction, rxn_005 should release H2O; rxn_006 should be serine nitrile + 2 H2O -> serine + NH3; rxn_007 should be split into explicit aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis or rewritten as a balanced net; rxn_010 needs H2O as product; rxn_002 and rxn_003 need proper photoredox stoichiometry and explicit UV with copper or UV with ferrocyanide context, with the sulfite branch better represented via a glycolaldehyde-sulfite adduct; and rxn_011 should not be a one-step clean serine synthesis. Best as written is P7 because rxn_008 is balanced and experimentally supported, but if rxn_005 and rxn_006 were fixed, the literature-favored glycolaldehyde Strecker route, especially P4 and then P1, would likely become the leading pathway. Overall this is a literature-aware but only moderately valid network, so it is not approved.\n- Issues: ['Conceptually strong and well aligned with the cyanosulfidic serine literature, but the implementation is under-specified. rxn_002 is unbalanced and omits the critical UV, CuCN and phosphate context; it likely needs H2O plus NH3 and S among the net species or should be split into photoredox and hydrolysis substeps. rxn_005 should produce H2O, and rxn_006 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3. The route also needs staged irradiation to avoid over-reduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde.', 'The sulfite branch is plausible but weaker for serine than the H2S branch because the cited literature supports the photoredox chemistry mainly to the aldehyde or aldehyde-sulfite-adduct stage. rxn_003 is unbalanced and should likely involve water plus NH3 and sulfate, or better a glycolaldehyde-sulfite adduct under UV and ferrocyanide. Downstream rxn_005 and rxn_006 need the same H2O and NH3 corrections as the direct Strecker route.', 'Classical formose-to-Strecker logic is chemically reasonable, but formose chemistry is unselective and poorly quantified for serine. rxn_004 needs the coefficient correction 2 CH2O -> glycolaldehyde and should specify base or mineral conditions. rxn_005 should release H2O and rxn_006 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3.', 'This is the strongest serine-specific route in the literature, but the implementation is incomplete. rxn_005 is missing H2O as product, and rxn_006 is missing one extra H2O reactant plus NH3 as product. If those stoichiometric fixes are made, this would likely become the best pathway in the network.', 'The final Cu-mediated hydroxymethylation is well supported and balanced, but the upstream glycine formation is too aggregated. rxn_007 fails conservation and should be decomposed into glycine-nitrile formation plus hydrolysis, or replaced by a balanced overall net. The Cu route should also note mild alkalinity, reversibility, and alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine as a competing product.', 'This route has the same upstream glycine-balance problem as P5, and the pyridoxal plus metal step is best treated as an experimental analogue rather than a strongly grounded prebiotic environment. rxn_009 is balanced and literature-supported as reversible chemistry, but the biochemical setting and pyridoxal availability are weakly justified.', 'As written, this is the cleanest serine-forming edge in the network: rxn_008 is balanced and experimentally supported. Confidence stays below approval because the pathway presupposes an existing glycine reservoir, Cu(II) and mildly alkaline aqueous conditions are not fully specified, and the reaction is reversible with known over-hydroxymethylation side products.', 'This is chemically plausible as reversible PLP-like model chemistry, but weaker prebiotically than P7. rxn_009 is balanced, yet the pathway depends on pyridoxal plus metal ions and a poorly defined biochemical environment rather than a well-motivated prebiotic setting.', 'Metabolism-congruent but still speculative. rxn_010 is unbalanced as written, likely needing H2O as product, omits the metal catalyst and environment used in analogous keto-acid aminations, and the cited literature explicitly notes that nonenzymatic serine formation from hydroxypyruvate has not been demonstrated.', 'Not viable as written. rxn_011 cannot conserve carbon because one formaldehyde cannot furnish C3 serine, and hydrothermal studies report only transient low-yield serine in complex mixtures, not a clean one-step conversion. This branch should be rewritten as a multistep, low-selectivity hydrothermal subnetwork or removed.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Serine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network prioritizes the two experimentally strongest terrestrial serine disconnections under mild, anoxic, near-surface aqueous conditions: (i) glycolaldehyde-centered Strecker chemistry, with glycolonitrile reduced to glycolaldehyde by H2S/CuCN, H2S alone, or sulfite/ferrocyanide before conversion to Ser-CN and hydrolysis; and (ii) glycine hydroxymethylation by formaldehyde under catalyst-gated Cu2+ or pyridoxal-metal conditions. Direct high-temperature hydrothermal serine formation, spark products, and exogenous delivery are not used as primary productive edges because the supplied literature treats them as low-selectivity or sink-dominated. Protonated H2SO3/H2SO4 formulas are bookkeeping stand-ins for sulfite/bisulfite and sulfate pools, and selectivity losses such as glycolaldehyde over-reduction and over-hydroxymethylation are treated in reaction reasoning rather than expanded as separate nodes to keep the network compact.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/S\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2SO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfurous acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"sulfite/bisulfite reservoir\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2SO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfuric acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"sulfate reservoir\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(3(5)6)1-7/h2,7H,1,4H2,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly alkaline aqueous pool; concentration by evaporation, freezing, or local ponding\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic cyanohydrin addition of HCN to formaldehyde gives glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile is the direct cyanohydrin of formaldehyde and the experimentally used C2 entry point to glycolaldehyde in cyanosulfidic chemistry (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). This condensation is chemically straightforward and creates the shared C2 nitrile reservoir used by the strongest serine-producing routes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"CuCN-assisted sulfide photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV; pH ~7 phosphate-buffered sulfidic pool; staged irradiation to limit over-reduction\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV/Cu(I)-assisted photoredox reduction of the nitrile followed by hydrolytic release of the aldehyde and ammonia, with sulfide serving as stoichiometric reductant.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the best-supported upstream step to serine: 254 nm irradiation of glycolonitrile with H2S and catalytic CuCN at pH ~7 gave glycolaldehyde hydrate in 42% yield after 2 h (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013), and Patel et al. (2015) embedded it in a broader cyanosulfidic network. The node is written as a net redox/hydrolysis step; key caveats are the local need for sulfide, copper, and UV, and over-reduction of glycolaldehyde on prolonged irradiation (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-free sulfide photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV; pH ~7 phosphate-buffered sulfidic pool; slower than the CuCN-assisted case\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct UV-driven sulfide reduction of the nitrile to an imine-equivalent followed by hydrolysis to glycolaldehyde and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson & Sutherland (2013) reported that the same H2S-driven photoreduction can proceed without copper, albeit more slowly, so this branch is chemically justified where CuCN is absent. It is lower-confidence than rxn_002 but avoids assuming persistent copper availability in every sulfidic pool.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ferrocyanide-assisted cyanosulfitic reduction to the glycolaldehyde stage\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrocyanide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ferrocyanide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation in a sulfite-rich near-neutral pool; free aldehyde likely buffered partly as sulfite adduct in reality\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV/ferrocyanide-assisted sulfite reduction of the nitrile to the aldehyde oxidation state, represented here as net release of glycolaldehyde and oxidation of sulfite to sulfate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Xu et al. (2018) demonstrated a cyanosulfitic alternative in which UV plus ferrocyanide and sulfite reduces nitriles to the glycolaldehyde stage, with 68% total reduced products after 1 h; experimentally the aldehyde is often trapped as a sulfite adduct. This reaction is therefore a compact net representation of reduction to the aldehyde reservoir, from which free glycolaldehyde can be released downstream.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde Strecker synthesis of serine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly basic aqueous pool; days to weeks; concentrated HCN and NH3 required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde imination/ammonolysis and cyanide addition give the alpha-aminonitrile Ser-CN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the literature-favored C3-forming step: glycolaldehyde undergoes Strecker chemistry with HCN/NH3 to give the stable aminonitrile Ser-CN in excellent yield (Foden et al., 2020), consistent with the integrated cyanosulfidic framework of Patel et al. (2015). No catalyst is required, but concentration of HCN and ammonia in shallow pools remains a geochemical constraint.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface hydrolysis of serine nitrile to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mild aqueous hydrolysis during wet-dry or long-residence surface cycling; avoid prolonged heating\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis release the amino acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Because Foden et al. (2020) identify Ser-CN as a stable aminonitrile reservoir, hydrolysis is the chemically necessary release step to free serine. Mild surface hydrolysis is favored because serine degrades rapidly at higher temperature by dehydration and fragmentation (Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada, 2009; Sato et al., 2004).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Geothermal-cycling hydrolysis of serine nitrile to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-345K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces under geothermal heating/cooling cycles; kept below destructive hydrothermal temperatures\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Repeated hydration in concentrated solutions promotes nitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis while minimizing high-temperature residence time.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A second plausible hydrolysis context is concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces undergoing geothermal cycling, where repeated hydration can drive Ser-CN hydrolysis without hot-vent exposure. This environment-specific step is somewhat speculative rather than directly quantified, so temperatures are kept below the rapid serine-loss regime noted by Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"classical Strecker setting in concentrated surface pools; neutral to mildly basic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde reacts with ammonia and cyanide to furnish the aminonitrile precursor to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminoacetonitrile is the classical Strecker product from formaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia and provides the glycine feeder branch to serine via hydroxymethylation. This feeder step follows the same aldehyde/HCN/NH3 logic emphasized in cyanosulfidic networks (Patel et al., 2015), although the serine-focused papers did not quantify it specifically.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"prolonged hydration in surface pools; mild conditions chosen to preserve downstream serine chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"General aminonitrile hydrolysis yields glycine with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Surface hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile furnishes glycine, enabling the Akabori serine route. This is general aminonitrile chemistry rather than a serine-specific highlighted experiment; Patel et al. (2015) and Foden et al. (2020) both treat aminonitriles as hydrolysis-competent reservoirs, and mild conditions are chosen to preserve downstream serine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-interface hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-345K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"concentrated pools or mineral interfaces under geothermal cycling; no high-temperature vent residence\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile hydration is promoted by repeated concentration and rehydration at prebiotic interfaces, releasing glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"An alternative glycine-releasing context is concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces undergoing geothermal cycling, which can accelerate nitrile hydration without invoking destructive high-temperature vent chemistry. This node is somewhat speculative for aminoacetonitrile specifically in the serine literature, but it follows general aminonitrile hydrolysis chemistry under the mild-temperature constraint emphasized by Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu2+-catalyzed hydroxymethylation of glycine to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly alkaline pool; reversible Akabori chemistry; alpha-hydroxymethylserine is a competing side product\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-glycinate mediated aldol-like hydroxymethylation of glycine by formaldehyde furnishes serine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the strongest non-Strecker serine-specific alternative: Akabori et al. (1959) obtained preparative DL-serine from glycine plus formaldehyde with catalytic CuSO4, and Chadha & Choughuley (1984) likewise observed serine in dilute mildly alkaline solution. The step is explicitly catalyst-gated because uncatalyzed coupling is too slow, and the reaction is reversible with alpha-hydroxymethylserine as a known competing product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyridoxal-Al3+-mediated hydroxymethylation of glycine to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyridoxal\", \"Al3+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyridoxal + Al3+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mineral-interface or concentrated-pool chemistry; reversible SHMT-like model system\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A pyridoxal Schiff-base with glycine undergoes metal-assisted C1 transfer/hydroxymethylation to give serine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Metzler, Longenecker & Snell (1954) showed that pyridoxal plus metal ions can reversibly mediate glycine + formaldehyde \u21cc serine, making this a biochemically congruent alternative to copper catalysis. It is prebiotically weaker than rxn_011 because pyridoxal itself lacks a secure abiotic source, so this edge is evidence-backed but contingent.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Route A1: CuCN-cyanosulfidic Strecker with surface hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Most strongly supported route: formaldehyde is trapped as glycolonitrile, reduced photochemically by H2S/CuCN to glycolaldehyde, converted to Ser-CN by Strecker chemistry, then hydrolyzed in surface water.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Route A2: CuCN-cyanosulfidic Strecker with geothermal-cycling hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Same upstream cyanosulfidic route as P1, but Ser-CN is released to serine in concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces under mild geothermal cycling.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Route A3: Copper-free sulfide photoredox Strecker with surface hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A slower H2S/UV variant that does not require copper, followed by the same glycolaldehyde Strecker chemistry and surface hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Route A4: Copper-free sulfide photoredox Strecker with geothermal-cycling hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Copper-free 