e\": 0.88,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.83,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.89,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.83,\n      \"feedback\": \"Reasonable abstraction of the sulfite and ferrocyanide branch, but rxn_004 is more compressed than the H2S routes. The balance check indicates rxn_004 should be mol_005 + 2 mol_003 + mol_009 -> mol_006 + mol_002 + mol_010, and the literature more directly supports a glycolaldehyde-sulfite adduct or reduced-product pool than immediate free glycolaldehyde. rxn_006 also needs 2 H2O.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Write rxn_004 as mol_005 + 2 mol_003 + mol_009 -> mol_006 + mol_002 + mol_010.\",\n        \"State that the literature primarily supports a glycolaldehyde-sulfite adduct or reduced-product pool and describe release to free glycolaldehyde before rxn_005.\",\n        \"Write rxn_006 with 2 mol_003 and include ferrocyanide and UV as catalyst metadata.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.97,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.71\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.8,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.84,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n      \"feedback\": \"This combines the most abstract upstream step with the least well-specified downstream environment. rxn_004 needs 2 H2O and better representation of the sulfite adduct chemistry; rxn_007 also needs 2 H2O and a real geothermal setting rather than Biochemical. As written, the route is plausible but underspecified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_004 and rxn_007 stoichiometries to include 2 mol_003 where required.\",\n        \"Replace Biochemical with a real geothermal or mineral environment and keep temperatures below strong serine decomposition regimes.\",\n        \"Explain how the sulfite adduct chemistry feeds the Strecker step.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.76\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.79,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.84,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Akabori-type branch is chemically real and literature-backed, but more catalyst-sensitive than the Strecker route. rxn_009 needs 2 H2O as written. rxn_011 should explicitly acknowledge reversibility and alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine as a common side product, and Cu2+ availability is contested under the network's anoxic near-surface framing.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Write rxn_009 as mol_012 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_013 + mol_002.\",\n        \"Specify mildly alkaline pH and a plausible Cu2+ source for rxn_011.\",\n        \"Note reversibility and alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine side production for the hydroxymethylation step.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.66\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.75,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.77,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.72,\n      \"feedback\": \"Glycine formation is fine, but the final pyridoxal-metal hydroxymethylation is the weakest productive edge prebiotically. rxn_009 needs 2 H2O. rxn_012 is known chemistry, but the catalyst system is poorly sourced here, is reversible, and the Biochemical label is not a physical environment.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Write rxn_009 with 2 mol_003.\",\n        \"Either justify a prebiotic pyridoxal source or downgrade rxn_012 to a speculative branch.\",\n        \"Replace Biochemical with a physical environment and note reversibility and over-hydroxymethylation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.74,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.76\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.76,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.83,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.61,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.81,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.75,\n      \"feedback\": \"Possible in principle, but the route mixes Surface to Biochemical to Surface with no transport or concentration argument. rxn_010 needs 2 H2O and should specify the mineral agent responsible for hydrolysis. rxn_011 remains reversible and side-product-prone, with Cu2+ availability still needing justification.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Write rxn_010 as mol_012 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_013 + mol_002.\",\n        \"Name the mineral interface and explain movement between the surface setting and that environment.\",\n        \"Specify a plausible Cu2+ source and include reversibility and side products for rxn_011.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.74,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.66\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.71,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.58,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.75,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n      \"feedback\": \"Weakest route overall. rxn_010 needs 2 H2O, and rxn_012 depends on a poorly justified pyridoxal-metal system in a vague Biochemical setting. Both the environmental specification and the catalyst sourcing need substantial strengthening before this branch can be considered robust.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Write rxn_010 with 2 mol_003.\",\n        \"Replace Biochemical with a specific mineral or aqueous setting and justify transport or concentration.\",\n        \"Justify pyridoxal-metal availability or treat rxn_012 as a low-confidence exploratory edge only.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"This is a connected, literature-aware serine network with two genuinely supported productive strategies: glycolaldehyde Strecker chemistry and catalyst-gated glycine hydroxymethylation. Network validity is good: all 12 reactions are balanceable and there are no isolated subgraphs, but rxn_004, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_009, and rxn_010 should be written with the extra H2O identified by the balance check, and mol_014's structure record should be repaired because its InChI did not parse. Literature alignment is strong overall: the network correctly prioritizes low-temperature surface or near-surface aqueous routes and appropriately treats hydrothermal, spark, and exogenous chemistry as secondary. Main remaining weaknesses are environmental specification and reaction compression: rxn_004 abstracts the sulfite and ferrocyanide branch beyond the directly demonstrated aldehyde-adduct stage, rxn_011 and rxn_012 omit reversibility and alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine side production, and several steps are assigned to a vague Biochemical environment with no explicit transport justification from Surface settings. Overall confidence is below the 0.9 approval threshold, but the core Strecker branch, especially P1 and P3, is strong.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.95, "rxn_002": 0.9, "rxn_005": 0.97, "rxn_006": 0.78}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.89, "pathway_coherence": 0.95, "environmental_progression": 0.91, "target_achievement": 0.92, "overall_confidence": 0.89, "feedback": "Best-supported route in the network. rxn_002 and rxn_005 closely match the strongest literature-backed cyanosulfidic serine sequence. Main correction is stoichiometric: rxn_006 should be written as mol_011 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_014 + mol_002. Surface hydrolysis is plausible, but aminonitrile hydrolysis may be slow and serine instability should be acknowledged.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_006"], "suggestions": ["Write rxn_006 explicitly as mol_011 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_014 + mol_002.", "Annotate rxn_002 with 254 nm UV, phosphate, pH about 7, and CuCN as catalyst metadata.", "If compactness allows, add acetaldehyde over-reduction and Ser-CN loss channels as optional sinks."]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.95, "rxn_002": 0.9, "rxn_005": 0.97, "rxn_007": 0.71}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.86, "pathway_coherence": 0.94, "environmental_progression": 0.76, "target_achievement": 0.87, "overall_confidence": 0.83, "feedback": "Upstream chemistry is strong, but the final hydrolysis step is weaker environmentally. rxn_007 also needs 2 H2O, and the title geothermal-cycling conflicts with the Biochemical environment label. If genuinely hot conditions are intended, serine degradation becomes a significant competing sink.", "approved": true, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_007"], "suggestions": ["Write rxn_007 explicitly as mol_011 + 2 mol_003 -> mol_014 + mol_002.", "Replace Biochemical with a physical prebiotic setting such as mild geothermal wet-dry cycling and specify temperature bounds.", "Explain transfer or concentration of Ser-CN from the surface photoredox setting