.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.83,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.8\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.88,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.93,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.84,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.9,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.89,\n      \"feedback\": \"Best-supported pathway. rxn_001 and rxn_004 match established cyanohydrin and Strecker chemistry, and rxn_002 is well aligned with cyanosulfidic photoreduction literature, but it should explicitly note UV irradiation and ideally CuCN/phosphate buffering; over-reduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde is also omitted. rxn_005 is chemically plausible but needs corrected stoichiometry: rxn_005 should be Ser-CN + 2 H2O -> serine + NH3. Overall this is the strongest serine route in the network and closest to literature consensus.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add UV, near-neutral aqueous conditions, and optional CuCN/phosphate to rxn_002.\",\n        \"Correct rxn_005 stoichiometry to include 2 H2O.\",\n        \"Add competing acetaldehyde formation from over-reduction and a serine degradation sink for better realism.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.8\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.72,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.74,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.86,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"The logic is coherent once glycolaldehyde exists, but rxn_003 is only a coarse shorthand for formose chemistry. It needs corrected stoichiometry, rxn_003: 2 CH2O -> glycolaldehyde, and explicit alkaline or mineral conditions; selectivity is a major known problem in the literature. rxn_004 and rxn_005 are strong downstream steps, though rxn_005 again needs 2 H2O. This route is plausible but clearly weaker than the cyanosulfidic upstream route.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_003 stoichiometry to 2 CH2O -> glycolaldehyde.\",\n        \"Specify base or mineral mediation for the formose step and acknowledge poor selectivity.\",\n        \"Correct rxn_005 to include 2 H2O.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.83,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.32\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.58,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.71,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.63,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.72,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.62,\n      \"feedback\": \"The upstream glycolaldehyde generation is sound, but rxn_006 over-compresses hydrothermal serine formation. As written it lacks NH3 or NH4+ even though Strecker-type amino acid synthesis needs an external amino source; literature hydrothermal systems also show serine to be transient and thermolabile. The surface-to-hydrothermal handoff is not physically justified in the pathway description.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Revise rxn_006 to include NH3 or NH4+ and treat it as low-yield, time-limited hydrothermal chemistry rather than a clean one-pot step.\",\n        \"Justify transfer of glycolaldehyde from a UV-lit surface setting into a hydrothermal environment.\",\n        \"Add serine decomposition to alanine or pyruvate under hot conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.32\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.45,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.6,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.55,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.6,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.51,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the weakest glycolaldehyde-centered route. rxn_003 is an unselective formose shorthand that needs 2 CH2O and explicit alkaline or mineral conditions, while rxn_006 is mechanistically under-specified because NH3 or NH4+ is missing and hydrothermal conditions rapidly degrade serine. Both steps are plausible only as very coarse net summaries, not as a clean target-directed path.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Replace rxn_003 with a more explicit glycolaldehyde-producing subnetwork or prefer the cyanosulfidic upstream route.\",\n        \"Revise rxn_006 to include NH3 or NH4+ and explicitly mark serine as transient under hydrothermal conditions.\",\n        \"If retained, present this pathway as speculative and include competing decomposition products.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.89,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.79,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"Strong alternative pathway. rxn_007 and rxn_008 are standard Strecker plus hydrolysis chemistry to glycine, although rxn_008 needs corrected stoichiometry: aminoacetonitrile + 2 H2O -> glycine + NH3. rxn_011 is literature-supported for serine formation, but only with explicit Cu2+ catalysis and mildly alkaline aqueous conditions; the catalyst, reversibility, and alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine side product are not modeled. Even so, this route is chemically credible and aligns well with published serine chemistry.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_008 stoichiometry to include 2 H2O.\",\n        \"Explicitly annotate Cu2+ and mildly alkaline aqueous conditions for rxn_011.\",\n        \"Add reversibility and alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine as a competing product in rxn_011.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.73\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.78,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.73,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.85,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Chemically plausible and supported in laboratory systems, but less secure prebiotically than the Cu2+ version. rxn_008 again needs 2 H2O. rxn_012 requires explicit pyridoxal plus a realistic metal ion such as Al3+, Cu2+, or Fe3+ under mildly alkaline aqueous conditions; without those cofactors the glycine plus formaldehyde coupling is too slow. The route is coherent, but catalyst realism is the main weakness.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_008 stoichiometry.\",\n        \"Specify the pyridoxal-metal cofactor set and justify its prebiotic availability.\",\n        \"Add reversibility and over-hydroxymethylation side products to rxn_012.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.4,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.84\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.56,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.73,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.56,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.76,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.63,\n      \"feedback\": \"The second step is reasonable with Cu2+, but rxn_009 is too compressed and likely mis-specified mechanistically: hydrothermal glycine synthesis in the literature typically involves NH3 or NH4+ or a more complex reaction network, whereas the reaction as written omits that amino source. The hydrothermal-to-surface transfer to Cu-catalyzed hydroxymethylation is also not justified. Literature support exists only for low-yield, transient hydrothermal amino acid formation.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Revise rxn_009 to include NH3 or NH4+ and treat it as low-yield hydrothermal net chemistry, not a clean elementary step.\",\n        \"Explain transport and quenching from hot hydrothermal fluid to a cooler Cu-bearing surface setting.\",\n        \"Add serine degradation channels at elevated temperature.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.4,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.73\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.5,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.69,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.51,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.73,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.58,\n      \"feedback\": \"This combines two under-specified elements: rxn_009 is a mechanistically weak hydrothermal net step lacking NH3 or NH4+, and rxn_012 depends on a contested pyridoxal-metal catalytic system that is not explicitly present. The route can be ima